{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>As the stars of the heavens and as the sand, etc.</b> We find that for times He likens Israel to stars, and for times to the sand that is upon the seashore, and for times to dust, as it says (Genesis 28:14) ״וְהָיָה זַרְעֲךָ כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ״. For all of these point to different times: in the time of tranquility and success He likens them to stars — a term of greatness, as Rashi explained on the verse ״ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם הִרְבָּה אֶתְכֶם וְהִנְּכֶם הַיּוֹם כְּכוֹכְבֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם לָרֹב״ (Deuteronomy 1:10) — He multiplied you and made you great. Likewise the הַרְבָּה אַרְבֶּה mentioned here is a term of greatness." "And the likeness to sand points to the time when the nations rise against Israel to destroy them yet are unable to overpower them — like the waves that swell as though they wanted to wash away the whole world, yet as soon as they reach the sand they break. So too the nations, as it says (Psalms 42:8) ״כָּל מִשְׁבָּרֶיךָ וְגַלֶּיךָ עָלַי עָבָרוּ״. To be sure, they could not overpower them, for there they fell and were broken; therefore He called them ״מִשְׁבָּרֶיךָ״ — your breakers. Therefore Israel was likened to this sand that breaks the waves, which cannot pass beyond the sand, for the sand is the statute and boundary of the sea. So the nations cannot destroy Israel. For this reason, when Esau came to meet Jacob, Jacob said in his prayer (Genesis 32:13) ״וְאַתָּה אָמַרְתָּ הֵיטֵב אֵיטִיב עִמָּךְ וְשַׂמְתִּי אֶת זַרְעֲךָ כְּחוֹל הַיָּם״. Why did he recall specifically the promise of the sand and not mention the stars, which carry a twofold goodness — both multitude and greatness? Nor did he mention a term of multitude, but rather ״וְשַׂמְתִּי זַרְעֲךָ כְּחוֹל הַיָּם״. Rather, because this is a promise that their enemies will not be able to overpower them — thus Esau would not be able to harm him. And for this reason he mentioned specifically the sand that is upon the seashore. For is there no other sand in the world besides it? Rather, because it is what breaks the waves as said above, therefore it says here ״וְכַחוֹל אֲשֶׁר עַל שְׂפַת הַיָּם״. ״וְיִרַשׁ זַרְעֲךָ אֵת שַׁעַר אֹיְבָיו״ — what does this have to do with that? Rather, it follows the way of 'not only this but also that' (לֹא זוֹ אַף זוֹ): for not only will they be like the sand, such that the waves — that is, the haters — cannot overpower them, but also this: that they shall possess the gate of their enemies and overpower them." "And the likeness to dust points to the time of lowliness, for in the time when they are like dust to be trodden under the lowest stratum, from there they will rise from the lowness of their state and break forth to every side, as it is written (Genesis 28:14) ״וְהָיָה זַרְעֲךָ כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ וּפָרַצְתָּ יָמָּה וָקֵדְמָה״, and as it is written (Psalms 44:26) ״כִּי שָׁחָה לֶעָפָר נַפְשֵׁנוּ״, and what is written after it? ״קוּמָה עֶזְרָתָה לָּנוּ״. And the reason for this: because Israel do not seek HaShem with all their heart except in the time when they are in the extremity of lowliness — as is known from the ways of all the generations, and our own. And perhaps in the likeness to dust He hinted at the Egyptian exile, when the Egyptians plowed over them like dust that one plows over. And this will be explained further on, in the portion of וַיֵּצֵא (Genesis 28:14), with the help of HaShem."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The closing phrase ״הַרְבָּה אַרְבֶּה אָמוּר כָּאן״ (\\\"I will greatly multiply, said here\\\") appears to allude back to the opening of Genesis 22:17 (וְהַרְבָּה אַרְבֶּה אֶת זַרְעֲךָ); rendered faithfully as a reference to the verse at hand.", 1 "The word מִשְׁבָּרֶיךָ is rendered 'your breakers' following the Kli Yakar's wordplay on שָׁבַר (to break), which the text makes explicit.", 2 "The phrase בִּדְיוֹטָה תַּחְתּוֹנָה (literally 'in the lowest chariot/layer') is translated hyper-literally as 'under the lowest stratum'; the word בְּדִיוֹטָה is obscure and possibly a corruption or loanword, rendered here per its contextual sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:32.403235934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}